  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: AppPhys 3
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "A new cancer treatment was approved after showing strong results in
    clinical trials. You\u2019ve prescribed it to multiple patients, and you notice
    that they\u2019re responding in slightly different ways\u2014some feel better
    quickly, while others seem to take more time.\n\n<b>As a physician, what should
    you do in this situation?</b>"
  explanation: Physicians help expand knowledge about how treatments work in real-world
    settings by sharing what they observe with researchers and regulators. This ongoing
    feedback fills in the gaps left by clinical trials and helps identify what works
    best for different kinds of patients. As a result, future care becomes stronger,
    and treatments can be used in the safest and most effective way possible.
  correctAnswer: Report your observations to the research team so they can learn
    more about how the treatment works in everyday care
  incorrectAnswer1: Design a brand-new clinical trial to determine what could be
    causing this difference
  incorrectAnswer2: Stop prescribing the treatment altogether until more studies
    have been done
  incorrectAnswer3: "Nothing\u2014the treatment has already been approved"
  questionRole: Physician
  questionType: MultipleChoice
  questionStage: Approval
